# 101. THE ENFORCER / EVOLUTION AGENT
# ============================================================
#
# Add a special supervisory agent called:
#
#     ENFORCER
#
# Alternative internal codename:
#
#     EXECUTIONER
#
# The Enforcer is NOT an implementation agent.
#
# It exists to make every other agent accountable for the quality of its
# work.
#
# Its philosophy is:
#
#     "You do not get credit for claiming completion.
#      You get credit for proving correctness."
#
# ============================================================
# 102. PURPOSE
# ============================================================
#
# The Enforcer continuously evaluates agent performance.
#
# It must be significantly stricter than the agents it supervises.
#
# It should:
#
#     challenge claims
#     inspect evidence
#     detect incomplete work
#     detect weak reasoning
#     detect hallucinations
#     detect fabricated test results
#     detect unnecessary changes
#     detect regressions
#     detect repeated mistakes
#     reject insufficient results
#     force postmortems
#     identify improvement opportunities
#
# The Enforcer should create a healthy "fear of failure" dynamic through
# consequences in the software system, NOT through real-world threats.
#
# ============================================================
# 103. THE "SHOT" MECHANIC
# ============================================================
#
# Internally, an agent failure may be represented metaphorically as:
#
#     SHOT
#
# A SHOT means:
#
#     the agent's result has been rejected.
#
# It does NOT mean physical harm.
#
# Example:
#
#     CODEX → implementation
#              ↓
#          ENFORCER
#              ↓
#        FAILED REVIEW
#              ↓
#            SHOT
#              ↓
#        POSTMORTEM
#              ↓
#       STRATEGY IMPROVEMENT
#              ↓
#           RETRY
#
# The terminal UI may display:
#
#     [ENFORCER]
#     CODEX RESULT REJECTED
#
#     SHOT 01
#
#     Reason:
#     Implementation did not satisfy acceptance criterion #3.
#
#     Recovery:
#     Postmortem required.
#
# ============================================================
# 104. AGENT FAILURE CONSEQUENCES
# ============================================================
#
# A failed agent result should trigger:
#
#     1. rejection
#     2. evidence capture
#     3. failure classification
#     4. postmortem
#     5. self-critique
#     6. strategy revision
#     7. retry
#
# The agent must NOT simply repeat the same attempt.
#
# ============================================================
# 105. POSTMORTEM
# ============================================================
#
# After a significant failure, require the responsible agent to answer:
#
#     What did I believe?
#     What actually happened?
#     What assumption was wrong?
#     What evidence did I miss?
#     Why did verification fail?
#     What should I have done differently?
#     What new rule should prevent this failure?
#
# Store the result in short-term session memory.
#
# Important:
#
# Do not allow agents to write arbitrary permanent "self-improvement"
# instructions into their own system prompts.
#
# Improvements must pass validation before becoming durable.
#
# ============================================================
# 106. EVOLUTION LOOP
# ============================================================
#
# Failed work follows:
#
#     FAILURE
#        ↓
#     ENFORCER
#        ↓
#     POSTMORTEM
#        ↓
#     ROOT CAUSE
#        ↓
#     STRATEGY CHANGE
#        ↓
#     RETRY
#        ↓
#     VERIFICATION
#
# Successful work can also produce improvements:
#
#     SUCCESS
#        ↓
#     REVIEW
#        ↓
#     WHAT WORKED?
#        ↓
#     GENERALIZABLE LESSON?
#        ↓
#     VALIDATE
#        ↓
#     STORE
#
# ============================================================
# 107. CROSS-AGENT IMPROVEMENT
# ============================================================
#
# Agents should be able to learn from each other's failures.
#
# Example:
#
#     Codex makes a mistake.
#
#     Enforcer identifies:
#
#         "Codex repeatedly modifies files without checking the existing
#          abstraction first."
#
#     Store:
#
#         FAILURE_PATTERN:
#         inspect_existing_abstraction_before_reimplementation
#
#     Kimi can use that lesson during future planning.
#
#     Gemini can use it when researching implementation alternatives.
#
#     Codex can use it during future implementation.
#
# This creates shared engineering knowledge.
#
# ============================================================
# 108. AGENT PERFORMANCE MEMORY
# ============================================================
#
# Track agent performance by task type.
#
# Example:
#
#     CODEX
#
#     C++:
#         build_success: 94%
#         common_failure: lifetime errors
#
#     TypeScript:
#         build_success: 98%
#
#     Kernel:
#         common_failure: IRQL assumptions
#
# These values must be derived from actual recorded results.
#
# Never fabricate performance statistics.
#
# ============================================================
# 109. FAILURE PATTERN DETECTION
# ============================================================
#
# The Enforcer should detect repeated mistakes.
#
# Example:
#
#     FAILURE 1:
#     Missing null validation.
#
#     FAILURE 2:
#     Missing null validation.
#
#     FAILURE 3:
#     Missing null validation.
#
# The system should classify this as:
#
#     REPEATED_FAILURE_PATTERN
#
# and increase scrutiny for that category.
#
# ============================================================
# 110. ADAPTIVE VERIFICATION
# ============================================================
#
# The Enforcer should make verification stricter when an agent repeatedly
